---
title: Classic Quiche Lorraine
description: A quick and authentic French Quiche Lorraine featuring a crisp shortcrust pastry shell filled with smoky diced bacon, tender onion rings, and a rich crème fraîche egg custard.
servings: 4
prep time: 25 minutes
cook time: 45 minutes
tags:
  - mains
  - french
  - baking
  - pork
  - comfort-food
source: https://www.tantefanny.com/rezepte/quiche-lorraine
---

= Preparation & Custard

Preheat the #oven{} to 190°C (375°F) conventional top/bottom heat, or 170°C (340°F) fan-assisted. Remove the @quiche dough{300%g}(1 package, fresh shortcrust or tart dough) from the refrigerator according to the package instructions so it can soften slightly before unrolling.

In a #medium mixing bowl{}, whisk together the @large eggs{4}, @crème fraîche{150%g}, and @fresh parsley{2%tbsp}(finely chopped) with a #whisk{} until smooth. Season the custard to taste with @freshly grated nutmeg{1%pinch}, @cayenne pepper{1%pinch}, @fine sea salt{1/2%tsp}, and @black pepper{1/2%tsp}(freshly ground).

= Assembly & Baking

Lightly grease a #fluted tart pan{}(about 26 to 28 cm in diameter) with @unsalted butter{1%tsp}. Unroll the @quiche dough{}, peel off the parchment paper, and press the dough gently into the pan, folding any overhanging edges inward to reinforce the rim.

> Note: Alternatively, you can place the dough directly into the tart pan together with the baking paper it came rolled in, which eliminates the need for greasing with butter. If you do not have a fluted tart pan, a ceramic baking dish or springform pan works just as well.

Scatter the @bacon{250%g}(diced into lardons) and @yellow onion{1}(peeled and sliced into thin rings) evenly across the bottom of the pastry shell. Pour the seasoned egg and crème fraîche mixture evenly over the filling.

Bake the quiche on the lowest rack of the #oven{} for ~{45%minutes} until the pastry is crisp and golden brown and the custard is puffed and set in the center. Allow to rest briefly before slicing and serving warm.
